insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
('sa-f-200','awssaa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is identity federation, and how does IAM Identity Center use SAML 2.0 for workforce access?$$,$$Identity federation lets users authenticate with an existing external identity provider (corporate AD, Okta, etc.) and receive temporary AWS credentials via STS instead of getting individual IAM users. IAM Identity Center (successor to AWS SSO) accepts SAML 2.0 assertions from that external IdP and grants federated access to one or more AWS accounts and roles from a single sign-on portal.$$),
('sa-f-201','awssaa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is attribute-based access control (ABAC) in IAM?$$,$$Access is decided by matching tags on the IAM principal against tags on the resource - e.g., a policy allows an action only if the principal's "project" tag equals the resource's "project" tag. It scales far better than creating a new role for every team or project (role-based access control) as the number of combinations grows.$$),
('sa-a-100','awssaa','acronym',1,'1.1',$$MFA$$,$$Multi-Factor Authentication - a second verification factor (hardware/virtual token, SMS) required beyond a password.$$),
('sa-f-202','awssaa','core',1,'1.2',$$How are Network ACL rules evaluated?$$,$$In order from the lowest rule number up; the first rule that matches the traffic is applied and evaluation stops there - so a lower-numbered explicit deny takes effect even if a higher-numbered rule would have allowed it. The default, unnumbered rule (*) denies anything matching no other rule.$$),
('sa-f-203','awssaa','core',1,'1.2',$$Why must a NACL have outbound rules for high-numbered ports even though inbound web traffic only uses port 443?$$,$$NACLs are stateless, so return traffic is not automatically permitted. The client's ephemeral source port (typically 1024-65535) becomes the destination port on the reply, so an explicit outbound rule allowing that ephemeral range is required or the responses are silently dropped.$$),
('sa-f-204','awssaa','core',1,'1.2',$$What does it mean to reference one security group as the source of another security group's rule?$$,$$Instead of a CIDR block, a security group rule can name another security group's ID as its source or destination. Any instance currently in that referenced group is automatically covered, so the rule keeps working as instances are added or removed - a common pattern for letting only the web tier reach the app tier.$$),
('sa-f-205','awssaa','core',1,'1.2',$$What are a new security group's default rules?$$,$$No inbound rules at all (every inbound request is denied until you add an allow) plus one default outbound rule permitting all outbound traffic. You typically add specific inbound allows and often tighten the outbound rule.$$),
('sa-f-206','awssaa','core',1,'1.2',$$How do you block traffic from one specific malicious IP address at the network layer without affecting anyone else?$$,$$Add an explicit deny rule for that IP in the subnet's Network ACL. Security groups only support allow rules, so they cannot deny a single bad actor - the NACL is the layer capable of an explicit block.$$),
('sa-f-207','awssaa','core',1,'1.2',$$Interface endpoint vs Gateway endpoint - what is the difference?$$,$$A Gateway endpoint (S3 and DynamoDB only) is a route-table target with no hourly charge. An Interface endpoint (most other services - Secrets Manager, SNS, SQS, KMS, Systems Manager, etc.) provisions an ENI with a private IP via AWS PrivateLink, bills hourly plus per-GB, and is also reachable from on-premises over Direct Connect or VPN.$$),
('sa-f-208','awssaa','core',1,'1.2',$$How does Systems Manager Session Manager improve on a bastion host for shell access?$$,$$It opens an interactive shell over the SSM agent with no inbound port 22 or 3389 open, no SSH keys to manage, and access controlled entirely by IAM policy with sessions logged to CloudWatch Logs or S3 - removing the bastion host as an attack surface entirely.$$),
('sa-a-101','awssaa','acronym',1,'1.2',$$NACL$$,$$Network Access Control List - a stateless, subnet-level firewall supporting explicit allow and deny rules.$$),
('sa-a-102','awssaa','acronym',1,'1.2',$$VPN$$,$$Virtual Private Network - here, Site-to-Site VPN, an IPsec-encrypted tunnel connecting an on-premises network to a VPC over the public internet.$$),
('sa-y-050','awssaa','feynman',1,'1.2',$$Explain, with a concrete incident-response example, why you need both security groups and NACLs even though security groups already filter traffic.$$,$$Model answer: Security groups are stateful and allow-only - great for saying "let the web tier talk to the app tier on port 8080," but they have no way to say "block this one address." If a specific IP starts hammering your app with malicious requests, a security group can't fix that; you'd have to remove the whole inbound rule, blocking everyone, not just the attacker. The subnet's NACL is stateless and supports explicit deny rules, so you add a low-numbered deny for that one IP - it's evaluated before any allow rule, blocks only that source, and everything else keeps working. That's the layered-defense reason both exist: security groups for "who's allowed" at the instance level, NACLs for "who's specifically blocked" at the subnet level.$$),
('sa-f-209','awssaa','core',1,'1.3',$$What are the three tiers of KMS keys and how do they differ?$$,$$AWS owned keys (used internally by a service, you never see or manage them, no cost), AWS managed keys (created automatically the first time you enable a service's default encryption, aws/service prefix, AWS rotates them yearly, free to use but you can't edit the key policy), and customer managed keys (created and fully controlled by you - custom key policy, optional rotation, cross-account sharing, small monthly fee).$$),
('sa-f-210','awssaa','core',1,'1.3',$$What is a KMS key policy, and why can't a broad IAM allow bypass it?$$,$$The key policy is the resource-based policy attached directly to the KMS key; it is the primary access control and must explicitly permit a principal's use of the key (or delegate that decision to IAM via the default policy). Even a principal with an IAM policy granting kms:* is denied if the key's own policy doesn't allow that principal - both must agree.$$),
('sa-f-211','awssaa','core',1,'1.3',$$What does automatic key rotation do for a customer managed KMS key, and how does it differ from Secrets Manager rotation?$$,$$Once enabled, KMS generates new cryptographic backing material for that key once a year automatically, while keeping all prior versions available so data already encrypted still decrypts fine - transparent to applications, since the key's ARN/alias never changes. This is a completely separate mechanism from Secrets Manager rotating the actual secret VALUE (like a database password) stored under a secret.$$),
('sa-f-212','awssaa','core',1,'1.3',$$When would you choose AWS CloudHSM over AWS KMS?$$,$$When you need a dedicated, single-tenant hardware security module you fully control - generating and managing your own key material with no AWS access to it - to meet strict compliance requirements like FIPS 140-2 Level 3. KMS also uses HSMs, but multi-tenant and AWS-operated.$$),
('sa-f-213','awssaa','core',1,'1.3',$$What is an S3 Bucket Key and what problem does it solve?$$,$$A short-lived, bucket-level copy of a data key derived from the KMS key, cached inside S3 to encrypt and decrypt many objects locally instead of calling KMS for every request. It cuts both the per-request KMS cost and the risk of hitting KMS API throttling for buckets doing SSE-KMS at high request volume.$$),
('sa-f-214','awssaa','core',1,'1.3',$$What does "EBS encryption by default" do?$$,$$An account/region-level setting that automatically encrypts every newly created EBS volume and every snapshot taken of it, using the default or a chosen KMS key - no need to remember to enable encryption on each volume individually.$$),
('sa-f-215','awssaa','core',1,'1.3',$$Can you enable encryption on an existing unencrypted RDS instance?$$,$$No - storage encryption can only be set when an RDS instance is created. To encrypt an existing instance you take a snapshot, copy that snapshot with encryption enabled, restore a new instance from the encrypted copy, and cut traffic over to it.$$),
('sa-f-216','awssaa','core',1,'1.3',$$How is data encrypted at rest in DynamoDB?$$,$$Every DynamoDB table is encrypted at rest by default. You choose the key tier: an AWS owned key (default, no cost), an AWS managed key (aws/dynamodb, visible in KMS, no extra cost), or a customer managed key (for a custom key policy, rotation control, and per-key CloudTrail auditing, at KMS pricing).$$),
('sa-f-217','awssaa','core',1,'1.3',$$Secrets Manager vs. Systems Manager Parameter Store SecureString - when would you pick each?$$,$$Secrets Manager costs per secret and API call but provides built-in automatic rotation (e.g., for RDS/Redshift credentials) and cross-account sharing - use it for credentials that must rotate. Parameter Store SecureString is essentially free and fine for static configuration values or secrets you are willing to rotate yourself; it has no native rotation scheduler.$$),
('sa-a-103','awssaa','acronym',1,'1.3',$$HSM$$,$$Hardware Security Module - a physical device that generates and protects cryptographic key material; backs both KMS and CloudHSM.$$),
('sa-a-104','awssaa','acronym',1,'1.3',$$CMK$$,$$Customer Master Key (KMS key) - a key you create and control in KMS, with your own key policy and optional rotation.$$),
('sa-a-105','awssaa','acronym',1,'1.3',$$SSE$$,$$Server-Side Encryption - encryption performed by the storage service itself (e.g., S3 SSE-S3/SSE-KMS/SSE-C) before writing data to disk.$$),
('sa-y-051','awssaa','feynman',1,'1.3',$$Explain why "KMS key rotation" and "Secrets Manager rotation" are two completely different things that people often confuse.$$,$$Model answer: They sound alike but rotate different things for different reasons. KMS key rotation replaces the cryptographic key material behind a customer managed key once a year, automatically, and keeps old material around so anything already encrypted still decrypts fine - you and your application never notice, because the key's ARN/alias never changes. Secrets Manager rotation is about the secret's VALUE - like a database password - being changed on a schedule via a Lambda function that also updates the database itself, so a stolen password stops working after the next rotation. One rotates the lock's internal mechanism invisibly; the other rotates the actual password you type. You can, and often should, use both together: a customer managed KMS key to encrypt the secret at rest inside Secrets Manager, while Secrets Manager separately rotates the credential value.$$);
insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
('sa-q-200','awssaa',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$Employees already authenticate against an on-premises Active Directory via SAML. The company wants them to get federated, temporary access to multiple AWS accounts from a single sign-on portal, without creating an individual IAM user for each employee. What should they configure?$q$,$q$["Create an IAM user for every employee","IAM Identity Center with the existing Active Directory as a SAML 2.0 identity source","Share the root account credentials with the team","One shared IAM role with no federation setup"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$IAM Identity Center integrates with an external SAML 2.0 identity provider (or Active Directory) to grant federated employees temporary, per-account role access from one SSO portal - avoiding per-employee IAM users, shared root use, or an undistinguished shared role.$q$),
('sa-q-201','awssaa',1,'1.1','mcq',3,$q$A company manages hundreds of project teams, each needing access only to resources for their own project, and does not want to keep creating a brand-new IAM role every time a project launches. Which IAM approach scales best?$q$,$q$["Attribute-based access control (ABAC) using a matching \"project\" tag on principals and resources","Creating a new IAM role for every project","Granting AdministratorAccess to every team","One shared IAM user for every team"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$ABAC ties access to matching tags (e.g., project=alpha) on both the principal and the resource, so a single policy scales across any number of projects without a new role per project. Role-per-project doesn't scale as project count grows, and blanket admin access or shared users violate least privilege.$q$),
('sa-q-202','awssaa',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$A Network ACL has rule 100 ALLOW inbound port 443 from 0.0.0.0/0 and rule 50 DENY inbound port 443 from a suspicious /24 range. Traffic arrives on port 443 from an address within that suspicious range. What happens?$q$,$q$["It is denied, because rule 50 is evaluated first and matches","It is allowed, because rule 100 covers all source addresses","Both rules apply, and the traffic is allowed with extra logging","Rule order does not matter for NACLs, only the highest rule number wins"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$NACL rules are evaluated in ascending rule-number order and the first match wins. Rule 50 is evaluated before rule 100, so the explicit deny for that suspicious range takes effect regardless of the broader allow at 100.$q$),
('sa-q-203','awssaa',1,'1.2','mcq',3,$q$Instances behind a NACL can send outbound HTTPS requests to the internet, but responses never arrive back, even though a stateful security group allows all outbound traffic. The NACL's inbound rules allow only port 443. What is missing?$q$,$q$["An inbound NACL rule allowing the ephemeral port range used for return traffic","A security group rule allowing port 443 outbound","An Internet Gateway route in the route table","A second NAT Gateway"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$NACLs are stateless, so return traffic for an outbound request must be separately allowed inbound on the ephemeral port range (typically 1024-65535) the OS chose as the source port. Restricting inbound to only port 443 blocks the responses even though the requests went out fine.$q$),
('sa-q-204','awssaa',1,'1.2','mcq',1,$q$Which design lets an application-tier security group automatically keep allowing traffic from web-tier instances as the web tier's Auto Scaling group adds and removes instances, without ever editing a rule?$q$,$q$["Reference the web tier's security group ID as the source in the app tier's inbound rule","Hardcode each web instance's private IP address in the app tier's security group","Use a NACL with the web subnet's CIDR block as the allowed source","Disable the application tier's security group entirely"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Referencing a security group as the source, instead of a CIDR or a list of IPs, automatically covers every instance currently in that group, so scaling the web tier up or down needs no rule changes. Hardcoded IPs would require constant maintenance.$q$),
('sa-q-205','awssaa',1,'1.2','mcq',1,$q$What are a newly created security group's default rules?$q$,$q$["Deny all inbound traffic, allow all outbound traffic","Allow all inbound traffic, allow all outbound traffic","Deny all inbound traffic, deny all outbound traffic","Allow all inbound traffic, deny all outbound traffic"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A new security group starts with no inbound rules at all, so every inbound request is denied until you add an allow, but it includes one default outbound rule permitting all outbound traffic.$q$),
('sa-q-206','awssaa',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$A security team must immediately stop all traffic from one specific IP address that is attacking a public web application, without affecting any other legitimate visitor. Which control achieves this?$q$,$q$["Add an explicit deny rule for that IP address in the subnet's Network ACL","Remove the security group's inbound HTTPS rule entirely","Add a deny rule for that IP address directly to the security group","Detach the Internet Gateway from the VPC"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Security groups only support allow rules and cannot deny one specific address; only a NACL, which supports explicit deny, can block a single IP while leaving everyone else unaffected. Removing the whole inbound rule or the gateway would block all users, not just the attacker.$q$),
('sa-q-207','awssaa',1,'1.2','mcq',1,$q$An application needs private, in-VPC connectivity to AWS Secrets Manager, which does not support Gateway VPC endpoints. What should be configured?$q$,$q$["An Interface VPC endpoint (AWS PrivateLink) for Secrets Manager","A Gateway VPC endpoint for Secrets Manager","A NAT Gateway only, with no endpoint","A public IP address on the calling instance"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Services other than S3 and DynamoDB expose private VPC connectivity through Interface endpoints, which are ENIs using PrivateLink - not Gateway endpoints, which are limited to those two services.$q$),
('sa-q-208','awssaa',1,'1.2','mcq',1,$q$A team wants engineers to get an interactive shell on production EC2 instances without opening inbound port 22, distributing SSH keys, or running a bastion host, while still centrally logging every session. What should they use?$q$,$q$["AWS Systems Manager Session Manager","A public bastion host with shared SSH keys","Opening port 22 to the office IP range","Storing SSH private keys in an S3 bucket"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Session Manager connects through the SSM agent and IAM, needs no open inbound ports or key distribution, and can log every session to CloudWatch Logs or S3 - eliminating the bastion host as an attack surface.$q$),
('sa-q-209','awssaa',1,'1.2','multi',2,$q$Select all statements that correctly distinguish security groups from Network ACLs. (Select all that apply)$q$,$q$["Security groups are stateful; Network ACLs are stateless","Network ACLs support explicit deny rules, while security groups only support allow rules","Security groups operate at the subnet level, while NACLs operate at the instance level","Network ACL rules are evaluated in ascending rule-number order, and the first match wins"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Security groups are stateful, allow-only, and apply at the instance level; NACLs are stateless, support both allow and deny, apply at the subnet level, and are evaluated in ascending rule-number order until a match. The third statement reverses those levels, which is incorrect.$q$),
('sa-q-210','awssaa',1,'1.2','matching',2,$q$Match each VPC connectivity or security concept to its correct description.$q$,$q${"left":["Interface VPC endpoint","Gateway VPC endpoint","Site-to-Site VPN","VPC peering connection"],"right":["ENI-based PrivateLink connectivity to most AWS services, billed hourly and per GB","Route-table-based private connectivity to S3 or DynamoDB only, at no extra hourly charge","An IPsec-encrypted tunnel between an on-premises network and a VPC over the public internet","A direct network connection between two VPCs that AWS does not encrypt by default"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Interface endpoints use PrivateLink ENIs for most services and are billed; Gateway endpoints are free route-table targets limited to S3/DynamoDB; Site-to-Site VPN encrypts on-premises-to-VPC traffic over the internet; VPC peering carries traffic between VPCs without AWS adding encryption, so sensitive peered traffic should add its own (such as TLS).$q$),
('sa-q-211','awssaa',1,'1.3','mcq',2,$q$An IAM role has a policy granting kms:Decrypt on all resources, but calls to decrypt using a specific customer managed KMS key still fail with AccessDenied. What is the most likely cause?$q$,$q$["The KMS key's key policy does not grant that principal permission to use the key","IAM policies are ignored entirely by KMS","The role needs kms:* instead of kms:Decrypt","KMS keys ignore all resource-based policies"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$KMS access requires both the identity-based IAM policy and the key's own resource-based key policy to permit the action. A broad IAM allow is still blocked if the key policy does not authorize that principal.$q$),
('sa-q-212','awssaa',1,'1.3','mcq',2,$q$A team needs a KMS key with a custom key policy restricting usage to specific roles, cross-account sharing with a partner account, and control over enabling scheduled key rotation. Which key type fits?$q$,$q$["An AWS owned key","A customer managed KMS key","An AWS managed key","A Secrets Manager secret with no associated key"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Only customer managed keys let you write a custom key policy, share the key cross-account, and opt into automatic rotation; AWS owned and AWS managed keys are controlled entirely by AWS with no editable key policy.$q$),
('sa-q-213','awssaa',1,'1.3','mcq',3,$q$A financial services firm must generate and store encryption key material in a single-tenant hardware module it fully controls, meeting FIPS 140-2 Level 3, with no possibility of AWS having any access to the key material. Which service meets this?$q$,$q$["AWS KMS with AWS managed keys","AWS CloudHSM","Amazon S3 with SSE-S3","Systems Manager Parameter Store"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$CloudHSM provides dedicated, single-tenant HSM instances that the customer fully controls, meeting FIPS 140-2 Level 3 requirements that shared, AWS-operated KMS does not guarantee at that level.$q$),
('sa-q-214','awssaa',1,'1.3','mcq',2,$q$An application performs millions of SSE-KMS encrypt and decrypt calls per day against one bucket and begins hitting KMS request throttling along with rising per-request KMS charges. Which AWS feature is designed to reduce this?$q$,$q$["S3 Bucket Keys","S3 Transfer Acceleration","S3 Requester Pays","Disabling encryption entirely"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$S3 Bucket Keys cache a short-lived data key at the bucket level so S3 can encrypt and decrypt many objects without calling KMS for every single request, cutting both KMS API cost and throttling risk.$q$),
('sa-q-215','awssaa',1,'1.3','mcq',1,$q$Which setting guarantees that every new EBS volume created in an account and region is automatically encrypted, without relying on each engineer remembering to enable it per volume?$q$,$q$["EBS encryption by default","S3 default encryption","RDS storage encryption","IAM permission boundaries"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$EBS encryption by default is an account/region setting that automatically encrypts every newly created volume and its snapshots with the chosen KMS key - no per-volume action required.$q$),
('sa-q-216','awssaa',1,'1.3','ordering',3,$q$Order the correct steps to add encryption at rest to an existing unencrypted RDS instance.$q$,$q$["Take a manual snapshot of the unencrypted instance","Copy the snapshot, enabling encryption on the copy","Restore a new DB instance from the encrypted snapshot copy","Update the application to point at the new encrypted instance and decommission the old one"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$RDS storage encryption can only be set at instance creation, so retrofitting it requires snapshotting the unencrypted instance, copying that snapshot with encryption enabled, restoring a new instance from the encrypted copy, and finally cutting the application over before decommissioning the old instance.$q$),
('sa-q-217','awssaa',1,'1.3','mcq',1,$q$By default, is data in a DynamoDB table encrypted at rest?$q$,$q$["No, encryption must be manually configured after table creation","Yes, every table is encrypted at rest by default using an AWS owned key unless a different key tier is chosen","Only Global Tables are encrypted by default","Only if a customer managed KMS key is explicitly attached"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$DynamoDB encrypts every table at rest by default at no extra charge using an AWS owned key, and lets you instead choose an AWS managed or customer managed KMS key for more visibility or control.$q$),
('sa-q-218','awssaa',1,'1.3','mcq',2,$q$A startup needs to store dozens of static configuration values and a handful of secrets, is cost-sensitive, and is fine handling rotation of those secrets manually. Which is the MOST cost-effective storage option?$q$,$q$["AWS Secrets Manager for everything","Systems Manager Parameter Store SecureString parameters","Plaintext values embedded in the application code","Amazon S3 with public read access"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Parameter Store SecureString parameters are essentially free and adequate when native automatic rotation is not required; Secrets Manager costs more per secret specifically for its built-in rotation, which is not needed here. Plaintext code and a public bucket are both insecure.$q$),
('sa-q-219','awssaa',1,'1.3','multi',3,$q$Select all TRUE statements about AWS KMS key deletion and S3 Object Lock. (Select all that apply)$q$,$q$["A KMS key marked for deletion enters a mandatory waiting period of 7 to 30 days before it is permanently deleted","KMS keys are deleted immediately upon request, with no waiting period","S3 Object Lock can enforce Write-Once-Read-Many (WORM) retention so objects cannot be deleted or overwritten for a set period","S3 Object Lock is normally enabled after a bucket already exists, never at bucket creation"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$KMS enforces a configurable 7-30 day pending-deletion window specifically to prevent accidental, irreversible data loss from premature key deletion. S3 Object Lock provides WORM retention (a retention period or legal hold) to block deletion or overwrite, and the normal way to use it is to enable it when the bucket is created - so the immediate-deletion and enable-after-creation statements are both false.$q$),
('sa-q-220','awssaa',1,'1.3','mcq',3,$q$A third-party billing system needs temporary, narrowly scoped permission to use a specific customer managed KMS key for one hour to decrypt a single batch job's data, without modifying that key's permanent key policy. Which KMS feature fits?$q$,$q$["A KMS grant","Deleting and recreating the key policy","Converting the key into an AWS managed key","Disabling the key temporarily"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$KMS grants provide temporary, revocable, fine-grained permission to use a key for a specific principal and set of operations without editing the key's persistent policy - ideal for short-lived, narrowly scoped delegation.$q$),
('sa-q-221','awssaa',1,'1.3','matching',2,$q$Match each data-at-rest control to its correct description.$q$,$q${"left":["KMS key policy","Customer managed KMS key rotation","AWS CloudHSM","RDS storage encryption"],"right":["Resource-based policy on the key that must permit a principal before any IAM allow can take effect","Annual replacement of a key's backing cryptographic material, transparent to applications still decrypting old data","Dedicated, single-tenant hardware security modules meeting the strictest compliance tiers","Must be enabled at instance creation; retrofitting it requires a snapshot, encrypted copy, and restore"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Each control addresses a distinct layer of data-at-rest protection: the key policy gates key usage, rotation refreshes key material without disrupting decryption, CloudHSM offers dedicated hardware for strict compliance, and RDS encryption is fixed at creation time only.$q$);
